<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Procopius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–c. 570</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Procopius of Caesarea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Procopius of Caesarea was a Byzantine historian of the 6th century and secretary to the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>. His 'History of the Wars' describes Justinian's campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and is the main source for many peoples on the empire's frontiers. He also wrote 'On Buildings' and the hostile 'Secret History'.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Procopius</b> insisted they were 'not nomads like the other Hunnic peoples' but settled under one king and a lawful constitution, while some Chinese sources said they lived in tents; Litvinsky sees nomads who moved into the cities they conquered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 520s King Gurgenes rose against Kavad I and appealed to the Byzantine Emperor Justin I, but when Persian armies invaded he fled with his family and nobles to Lazica, according to <b>Procopius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 429 the Vandals, under their new king Gaiseric, crossed from Hispania to Africa; <b>Procopius</b> put their number at 80,000, a figure some historians doubt, and Peter Heather estimates an army of 15,000 to 20,000.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Procopius</b> wrote in the 6th century that they held the maker of lightning to be lord over all and sacrificed bulls to him.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The court historian <b>Procopius</b> wrote that the rioters had taken up arms “no less against God himself”, and that God had permitted it, foreseeing the beauty the shrine was destined to take on.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Antes appear in the 6th century in <b>Procopius</b> and Jordanes, who describe them as a people related to the Sclaveni living east of the Dniester.</blockquote><a href="/state/antes">Antes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Caesarea Maritima, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.5,34.89&amp;year=545" aria-label="Caesarea Maritima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5446 982 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Procopius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5896 1252h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5896 1252h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.5,34.89&amp;year=545">What was here in 545 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
